ORGO.reg({"types":{"mech-two-paths":{"id":"mech-two-paths","title":"Two conditions, one product: E2 and E1 mechanisms, and what the NMR says","exam":"Q2B","points":70,"asks":"One substrate gives the same product under two sets of conditions. Draw both mechanisms and interpret a clue from the spectrum.","know":[{"h":"<b>The reagent picks the mechanism.</b> The same halide can lose HX in one step or in two. A strong base does not wait: it pulls off a hydrogen while the leaving group leaves ({{E2}}). With only a weak base (the solvent), the leaving group must leave first ({{E1}}).","from":"Lecture 11/10, p. 4 and 11/12, p. 1"},{"h":"<b>Strong bases to recognize</b>: {{HO-}}, alkoxides ({{CH3O-}}, {{CH3CH2O-}}, {{(CH3)3CO-}}), {{H2N-}}, and hydride {{H-}} (NaH is {{Na+}} and {{H-}}). <b>Weak bases</b>: {{H2O}} and alcohols ({{CH3OH}}, {{CH3CH2OH}}) used as the solvent with nothing added.","from":"Lecture 10/29, p. 1 and 11/12, p. 2"},{"h":"<b>{{E2}}</b>: one concerted step, no intermediate, three arrows. Base lone pair to the hydrogen; the C-H bond to the new C=C; the C-LG bond to the leaving group. rate = k[RX][base]. The hydrogen and the leaving group are anti-periplanar.","from":"Lecture 11/12, p. 1 to 3"},{"h":"<b>{{E1}}</b>: two steps. Step 1 (slow): the C-LG bond breaks and leaves a carbocation. Step 2: the weak base takes a hydrogen from a carbon next to the positive carbon, and the C-H electrons form the C=C. rate = k[RX].","from":"Lecture 11/10, p. 4"},{"h":"<b>The carbocation must be stable</b> for {{E1}} or {{SN1}}: tertiary is good, secondary is possible, primary and methyl never form. A neighbouring benzene ring or C=C stabilizes it by resonance.","from":"Lecture 11/10, p. 2; flow chart"},{"h":"<b>{{E1}} and {{SN1}} share the carbocation</b>, so a solvent-only flask always holds some substitution product too: an alcohol in water, an ether in an alcohol. Higher temperature favours {{E1}}, lower temperature favours {{SN1}}.","from":"Lecture 11/10, p. 4"},{"h":"<b>{{SN2}} never happens at a tertiary carbon</b> (relative rate 0), so a strong base with a tertiary halide gives {{E2}} only. That is why {{NaOH}} with tert-butyl bromide gives the alkene and no alcohol.","from":"Lecture 11/10, p. 1 and 11/12, p. 1"},{"h":"<b>On a secondary carbon the pair is {{SN2}} and {{SN1}}</b>: a good nucleophile that is a weak base (such as {{HS-}}) in a polar aprotic solvent attacks from the back in one step and <b>inverts</b> the carbon. The solvent alone (water) lets the leaving group ionize first, and the planar cation is attacked on either face: a <b>racemic</b> product, formed in three steps (ionize, attack, lose {{H+}}).","from":"Lecture 11/03, p. 2 to 4 and 11/10, p. 3"},{"h":"<b>Tosylate ({{OTs}})</b> is a good leaving group, like bromide or iodide.","from":"Lecture 11/05, p. 2"},{"h":"<b>Where the base ends up.</b> In {{E2}} the base leaves bonded to the hydrogen it took: {{H-}} becomes {{H2}}, {{CH3O-}} becomes {{CH3OH}}, {{HO-}} becomes {{H2O}}. In {{E1}} the neutral solvent becomes {{H3O+}} or {{CH3OH2+}}.","from":"FA24 Exam 2, Q2B"},{"h":"<b>The n + 1 rule.</b> A hydrogen with n equivalent hydrogens on the neighbouring carbon appears as n + 1 lines: no neighbours, a singlet; 1, a doublet; 2, a triplet; 3, a quartet; 6, a septet.","from":"Lecture 10/22, p. 3; FA24 Exam 2, Q2B"},{"h":"<b>Counting signals.</b> Hydrogens in the same environment give one signal, so a very symmetrical molecule gives very few signals. An electronegative atom (O, F) pulls electron density away from the hydrogens near it: they are less shielded and their signal stands apart.","from":"Lecture 10/22, p. 2 to 3"},{"h":"<b>A small extra signal means a by-product.</b> Ask what else the reagent could have done. A strong base can also act as a nucleophile (hydride replacing the halide by H). A solvent can also attack the carbocation ({{SN1}}). Draw the by-product and check that it explains the signal.","from":"FA24 Exam 2, Q2B"},{"h":"<b>Energy diagrams.</b> {{E2}} and {{SN2}}: one hump. {{E1}} and {{SN1}}: two humps with the carbocation in the valley, and the first hump (ionization) is the higher one.","from":"Lecture 11/10, p. 2 and 11/12, p. 1"}],"method":["Classify the carbon that carries the leaving group (tertiary, secondary, benzylic) and mark the hydrogens on the carbons next to it.","Read each set of conditions and label the reagent: strong base, good nucleophile that is a weak base, or solvent alone.","For the strong base, draw one step with three arrows (E2). For a good nucleophile on a secondary carbon, draw one step with two arrows and invert the carbon (SN2).","For the solvent alone, break the bond to the leaving group first and draw the carbocation. Then draw the second step: the solvent takes a neighbouring hydrogen (E1), or attacks the positive carbon and then loses a proton (SN1).","Write every product of each flask, including what the base and the leaving group became.","Turn the spectral clue into a structure: lines minus one is the number of neighbouring hydrogens, and the number of signals tells you the symmetry. Draw the fragment that fits.","Ask which reagent could have made that fragment and by which reaction, and say why the other flask cannot make it."],"pitfalls":[{"h":"Drawing a carbocation in the strong-base reaction. E2 has no intermediate: all three arrows go in one drawing."},{"h":"Using {{HO-}} or {{CH3O-}} as the base in the solvent-only reaction. Only the neutral solvent is in that flask, and it leaves as {{H3O+}} or {{CH3OH2+}}."},{"h":"Sending the base or nucleophile into a tertiary carbon. SN2 does not happen there."},{"h":"Starting an arrow at a hydrogen atom or at a positive charge. Arrows start at electrons: a lone pair or a bond."},{"h":"Reading a septet as seven hydrogens. It is one kind of hydrogen with six neighbours."},{"h":"Stopping the SN1 mechanism at the protonated alcohol, or drawing only one enantiomer of the product."}],"links":{"read":["sn1-e1","e2","nmr","choose"]}}},"items":{"exam-q2b-written":{"id":"exam-q2b-written","type":"mech-two-paths","role":"exam","kind":"written","diff":3,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q2B","time":12,"pts":70,"stem":"2-Iodo-2-phenylpropane gives the same major product, Product A, under two different conditions.\n[[rxn:CC(C)(I)c1ccccc1>NaH>\"Product A\"]]\n[[rxn:CC(C)(I)c1ccccc1>H2O>\"Product A\"]]\n<p>(a) Draw Product A and a mechanism for each reaction.</p><p>(b) The crude {{1H}} NMR spectrum of the NaH reaction shows a small septet. What does that tell you?</p>","model":"<p><b>Product A</b> is the alkene 2-phenylpropene: [[mol:C=C(C)c1ccccc1]]</p><p><b>With NaH: {{E2}}, one step.</b> Hydride is a strong base and the halide is tertiary, so {{SN2}} is impossible. Hydride takes a hydrogen from a {{CH3}}, the C-H electrons form the C=C, and iodide leaves. Three arrows. The other products are {{H2}} and NaI.</p>\n[[mech:e2-cumyl-iodide-hydride]]\n<p><b>With {{H2O}}: {{E1}}, two steps.</b> Water is a weak base, so the C-I bond ionizes first (the slow step) to a carbocation that is tertiary and benzylic. Water then takes a hydrogen from a {{CH3}} and the C-H electrons form the C=C. The other products are {{H3O+}} and {{I-}}.</p>\n[[mech:e1-cumyl-iodide-water]]\n<p><b>The septet.</b> Seven lines mean a hydrogen with six equivalent neighbours (n + 1 = 7): the CH of an isopropyl group, {{(CH3)2CH}}. So the crude product contains a little isopropylbenzene: [[mol:CC(C)c1ccccc1]]</p><p>A small part of the hydride acted as a <b>nucleophile</b> and replaced iodide with H, instead of acting as a base. In water there is no hydride, so this by-product cannot form there.</p>","rubric":[{"pts":25,"h":"NaH reaction drawn as E2 in one step with three correct arrows: hydride to H, C-H bond to the new C=C, C-I bond to I"},{"pts":25,"h":"Water reaction drawn as E1 in two steps: ionization to the carbocation (drawn, with its charge), then water removing a neighbouring H with the C-H electrons forming the C=C"},{"pts":5,"h":"Product A drawn as 2-phenylpropene"},{"pts":8,"h":"Septet read correctly: a hydrogen with six equivalent neighbours, the CH of an isopropyl group"},{"pts":7,"h":"Inference: some hydride acted as a nucleophile and gave isopropylbenzene as a minor product"}],"solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Classify the substrate","h":"The carbon that carries iodine has two methyl groups and a phenyl: tertiary, and benzylic. No SN2, ever. Iodide is an excellent leaving group."},{"t":"Find the product both conditions share","h":"Neither set of conditions can substitute cleanly at a tertiary carbon by SN2, and the two reagents have nothing in common except that each can remove a proton. Loss of HI gives the alkene [[mol:C=C(C)c1ccccc1]]. The beta hydrogens are all on the two methyl groups, so there is only one alkene."},{"t":"NaH: strong base, so E2","h":"{{H-}} is a very strong base (it gives {{H2}}). Strong base plus tertiary halide: one concerted step, three arrows."},{"t":"Water: weak base, so E1","h":"Nothing pushes the iodide out, so the molecule ionizes first. The cation is tertiary and shares its charge with the ring by resonance, and water stabilizes the ions. Then water removes a hydrogen from a {{CH3}}. Draw the carbocation: it is worth points. Water attacking the cation instead would give the SN1 alcohol; the question says the alkene is major, and it is conjugated with the ring."},{"t":"Read the septet","h":"The n + 1 rule: 7 lines means 6 equivalent neighbouring hydrogens, two {{CH3}} groups on one CH. Product A has no such hydrogen (its {{CH3}} has no hydrogen next door). An isopropyl group must have appeared."},{"t":"Explain where it came from","h":"The only source of a new H on that carbon is hydride. A little hydride attacked carbon (substitution) and gave isopropylbenzene. Small signal: minor product."}],"answer":"Product A is 2-phenylpropene. NaH: E2 (one step, three arrows). Water: E1 (ionization to a tertiary benzylic cation, then loss of a proton to water). The septet is the CH of an isopropyl group: a little isopropylbenzene formed because some hydride acted as a nucleophile.","rule":"Same alkene, two mechanisms: a strong base gives E2 in one step, a weak base waits for the carbocation (E1)."}},"exam-q2b-e2":{"id":"exam-q2b-e2","type":"mech-two-paths","role":"exam","kind":"mech","diff":2,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q2B","mech":"e2-cumyl-iodide-hydride","stem":"Draw the curved arrows for the reaction of 2-iodo-2-phenylpropane with sodium hydride.","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Decide the mechanism","h":"Tertiary halide and a strong base ({{H-}}): E2, one step."},{"t":"Draw the three arrows","h":"Hydride lone pair to a hydrogen of a {{CH3}}. That C-H bond to the bond between the {{CH3}} carbon and the carbon bearing iodine. The C-I bond to iodine."}],"answer":"One step, three arrows. Products: 2-phenylpropene, {{H2}} and iodide.","rule":"E2: base to H, C-H to the pi bond, C-LG to the leaving group, all at once."}},"exam-q2b-e1":{"id":"exam-q2b-e1","type":"mech-two-paths","role":"exam","kind":"mech","diff":2,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q2B","mech":"e1-cumyl-iodide-water","stem":"Draw the curved arrows for the reaction of 2-iodo-2-phenylpropane in water that gives the alkene.","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Decide the mechanism","h":"Tertiary, benzylic halide and only a weak base (water): the leaving group leaves first. E1, two steps."},{"t":"Step 1: one arrow","h":"The C-I bond to iodine. Draw the carbocation that results."},{"t":"Step 2: two arrows","h":"A lone pair of water to a hydrogen on a {{CH3}} next to the positive carbon, and that C-H bond to the bond between the two carbons."}],"answer":"Two steps, three arrows, with a tertiary benzylic carbocation between them.","rule":"E1: ionize first, then a weak base removes a neighbouring hydrogen."}},"mech-id-s02":{"id":"mech-id-s02","type":"mech-two-paths","kind":"mech","diff":2,"src":"Lecture 11/12","mech":"e2-tertbutyl-bromide-hydroxide","stem":"Draw the curved arrows for the reaction of tert-butyl bromide with hydroxide.","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Decide the mechanism","h":"Tertiary carbon and a strong base: E2."},{"t":"Draw the three arrows","h":"Base to hydrogen; C-H bond to the new pi bond; C-Br bond to bromine."}],"answer":"One step, three arrows","rule":"E2: three arrows at once."},"role":"practice"}},"mols":{"CC(C)(I)c1ccccc1":{"w":153,"h":74,"p":[[0,"M 102.7,8.9 L 102.7,36.9",0],[1,"M 102.7,36.9 L 102.7,64.9",0],[2,"M 102.7,36.9 L 126.4,36.9",0],[3,"M 102.7,36.9 L 74.7,36.9",0],[4,"M 74.7,36.9 L 60.7,12.6",0],[4,"M 69.8,36.9 L 58.3,16.8",0],[5,"M 60.7,12.6 L 32.7,12.6",0],[6,"M 32.7,12.6 L 18.7,36.9",0],[6,"M 35.1,16.8 L 23.5,36.9",0],[7,"M 18.7,36.9 L 32.7,61.1",0],[8,"M 32.7,61.1 L 60.7,61.1",0],[8,"M 35.1,56.9 L 58.3,56.9",0],[9,"M 60.7,61.1 L 74.7,36.9",0],[-1,"M 61.4,13.8 L 60.7,12.6 L 59.3,12.6",0],[-1,"M 34.1,12.6 L 32.7,12.6 L 32.0,13.8",0],[-1,"M 19.4,35.7 L 18.7,36.9 L 19.4,38.1",0],[-1,"M 32.0,59.9 L 32.7,61.1 L 34.1,61.1",0],[-1,"M 59.3,61.1 L 60.7,61.1 L 61.4,59.9",0]],"t":[[3,127.3,42.5,11,"I"]],"a":[[102.7,8.9,"C",0,0,3,0],[102.7,36.9,"C",0,0,0,0],[102.7,64.9,"C",0,0,3,0],[130.7,36.9,"I",0,3,0,0],[74.7,36.9,"C",0,0,0,0],[60.7,12.6,"C",0,0,1,0],[32.7,12.6,"C",0,0,1,0],[18.7,36.9,"C",0,0,1,0],[32.7,61.1,"C",0,0,1,0],[60.7,61.1,"C",0,0,1,0]],"b":[[0,1,1],[1,2,1],[1,3,1],[1,4,1],[4,5,2],[5,6,1],[6,7,2],[7,8,1],[8,9,2],[9,4,1]]},"C=C(C)c1ccccc1":{"w":132,"h":65,"p":[[0,"M 115.2,9.9 L 100.8,34.9",0],[0,"M 111.6,7.8 L 97.2,32.8",0],[1,"M 99.6,32.8 L 113.4,56.8",0],[2,"M 99.6,32.8 L 71.9,32.8",0],[3,"M 71.9,32.8 L 58.0,8.8",0],[3,"M 67.1,32.8 L 55.6,13.0",0],[4,"M 58.0,8.8 L 30.3,8.8",0],[5,"M 30.3,8.8 L 16.5,32.8",0],[5,"M 32.7,13.0 L 21.3,32.8",0],[6,"M 16.5,32.8 L 30.3,56.8",0],[7,"M 30.3,56.8 L 58.0,56.8",0],[7,"M 32.7,52.6 L 55.6,52.6",0],[8,"M 58.0,56.8 L 71.9,32.8",0],[-1,"M 100.2,34.0 L 99.6,32.8 L 98.2,32.8",0],[-1,"M 58.7,10.0 L 58.0,8.8 L 56.6,8.8",0],[-1,"M 31.7,8.8 L 30.3,8.8 L 29.6,10.0",0],[-1,"M 17.2,31.6 L 16.5,32.8 L 17.2,34.0",0],[-1,"M 29.6,55.6 L 30.3,56.8 L 31.7,56.8",0],[-1,"M 56.6,56.8 L 58.0,56.8 L 58.7,55.6",0]],"t":[],"a":[[113.4,8.8,"C",0,0,2,0],[99.6,32.8,"C",0,0,0,0],[113.4,56.8,"C",0,0,3,0],[71.9,32.8,"C",0,0,0,0],[58,8.8,"C",0,0,1,0],[30.3,8.8,"C",0,0,1,0],[16.5,32.8,"C",0,0,1,0],[30.3,56.8,"C",0,0,1,0],[58,56.8,"C",0,0,1,0]],"b":[[0,1,2],[1,2,1],[1,3,1],[3,4,2],[4,5,1],[5,6,2],[6,7,1],[7,8,2],[8,3,1]]},"CC(C)c1ccccc1":{"w":130,"h":64,"p":[[0,"M 113.1,55.7 L 99.2,31.7",0],[1,"M 99.2,31.7 L 113.1,7.7",0],[2,"M 99.2,31.7 L 71.5,31.7",0],[3,"M 71.5,31.7 L 57.7,7.7",0],[3,"M 66.7,31.7 L 55.3,11.8",0],[4,"M 57.7,7.7 L 30.0,7.7",0],[5,"M 30.0,7.7 L 16.1,31.7",0],[5,"M 32.4,11.8 L 20.9,31.7",0],[6,"M 16.1,31.7 L 30.0,55.7",0],[7,"M 30.0,55.7 L 57.7,55.7",0],[7,"M 32.4,51.5 L 55.3,51.5",0],[8,"M 57.7,55.7 L 71.5,31.7",0],[-1,"M 58.4,8.9 L 57.7,7.7 L 56.3,7.7",0],[-1,"M 31.3,7.7 L 30.0,7.7 L 29.3,8.9",0],[-1,"M 16.8,30.5 L 16.1,31.7 L 16.8,32.9",0],[-1,"M 29.3,54.5 L 30.0,55.7 L 31.3,55.7",0],[-1,"M 56.3,55.7 L 57.7,55.7 L 58.4,54.5",0]],"t":[],"a":[[113.1,55.7,"C",0,0,3,0],[99.2,31.7,"C",0,0,1,0],[113.1,7.7,"C",0,0,3,0],[71.5,31.7,"C",0,0,0,0],[57.7,7.7,"C",0,0,1,0],[30,7.7,"C",0,0,1,0],[16.1,31.7,"C",0,0,1,0],[30,55.7,"C",0,0,1,0],[57.7,55.7,"C",0,0,1,0]],"b":[[0,1,1],[1,2,1],[1,3,1],[3,4,2],[4,5,1],[5,6,2],[6,7,1],[7,8,2],[8,3,1]]}}});
